{:edb/db-before-t 1850 :edb/db-after-t 1851 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535163 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:23:08.720Z" 13194139535163 true] [17592186179456 1100 "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2$ along the quarter-circle $x^2+y^2=4$, traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant." 13194139535163 false] [17592186179456 1100 "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2$ along the quarter-circle $x^2+y^2=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant." 13194139535163 true] [17592186179456 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/d0ae53dd5d0932cbf834a166e851c227f75cb546b591a9fd43930ec06f0e9ddf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535163 true] [17592186179456 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3688/Source/Images/ae378f2d7290f1c5b6d5c6b3f26dd450.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2\\sin t)^{2} + (2\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x+y^2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 2(2\\cos t) + (2\\sin t)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4(\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)(2x + y^{2})ds &= ∫_{π/2}^{π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/2}^{π}4(\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t) \\cdot 2 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 8∫_{π/2}^{π}(\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535163 false] [17592186179502 1100 "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x^2-1$, traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1$." 13194139535163 false] [17592186179502 1100 "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x^2-1,$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1.$" 13194139535163 true] [17592186179502 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t-1)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t-1\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535163 true] [17592186179502 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (\\cos t)^{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} t - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{2π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos^{2} t - 1) \\cdot 1 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos^{2} t - 1dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535163 false] [17592186179509 1100 "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y) =x^2-2$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 4$." 13194139535163 false] [17592186179509 1100 "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y) =x^2-2$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$" 13194139535163 true] [17592186179509 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2-2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-2 \\\\  & =2(2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535163 true] [17592186179509 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 2\\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2\\sin t)^{2} + (2\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4\\sin^{2} t + 4\\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2-2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (2\\cos t)^{2} - 2 \\\\\n&= 4\\cos^{2} t - 2 \\\\\n&= 2(2\\cos^{2} t - 1) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{2π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}2\\cos 2t \\cdot 2 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos 2tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t$,\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos 2tdt &= 2∫_{0}^{4π}\\cos udu \\\\\n&= 2\\sin u \\mid _{0}^{4π} \\\\\n&= 2(0 - 0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535163 false] [17592186179601 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334823 13194139535163 true] [17592186179601 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334830 13194139535163 true] [17592186179601 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334837 13194139535163 true] [17592186179601 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334851 13194139535163 true] [17592186179601 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334865 13194139535163 true] [17592186179640 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334795 13194139535163 true] [17592186179640 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334802 13194139535163 true] [17592186179640 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334809 13194139535163 true] [17592186179640 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334816 13194139535163 true] [17592186179647 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334788 13194139535163 true] [17592186179647 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334844 13194139535163 true] [17592186179647 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334858 13194139535163 true] [17592186179888 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535163 true] [17592186179888 1100 "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - y^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$." 13194139535163 false] [17592186179888 1100 "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equatio