 point $(3,0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" 13194139535163 true] [17592186334865 1113 #uuid "b42e9373-4893-5f93-a4eb-1c63f18b5e00" 13194139535163 true] [17592186334865 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/b7c2c6f9e50988629007a06213cb3acf0a48f9951f026fb70d5a986b760abdfe.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9-9{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{alig